# Introduction

This HW will go through much of the same workflow we've demonstrated in class.
The main goal is to reinforce our demo of R and RStudio, which we will be using throughout the course both to learn the statistical concepts and to analyze real data and come to informed conclusions.
Many of the things written in here, we've learned in class and should feel like review.

::: callout-note
R is the name of the programming language itself and RStudio is a convenient interface.
:::

## Learning goals

By the end of this homework, you will...

- Be familiar with the workflow using RStudio
- Gain practice writing a reproducible report using Quarto
- Be able to create data visualizations using `ggformula` and use those visualizations to describe distributions

# Getting Started

### Log in to RStudio

- Go to RStudio and login with your College of Idaho Email and Password.

- Make a folder called `hw`to store all of your homework for the semester. Create a subfolder called `hw01` to store this homework.

- Log into [Canvas](https://cofi.instructure.com/courses/18743), navigate to HW-01 and upload the `hw-01.qmd` file into the folder you just made. Unlike some future homeworks, there is no separate data file to upload: today's data loads directly from the internet, so you'll need to be online when you render.

## R and RStudio

See the [R resources](/computing-r-resources.qmd) page for a guide to the RStudio IDE and Quarto file components.

### YAML

The top portion of your Quarto file (between the three dashed lines) is called **YAML**. It stands for "Yet Another Markup Language". It is a human friendly data serialization standard for all programming languages. All you need to know is that this area is called the YAML (we will refer to it as such) and that it contains meta information about your document.

# Packages

We will use the following packages in today's HW. The line `eval: FALSE` prevents R from running the corresponding chunk. As you work through this HW please delete these lines as you go.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(mosaic)
library(ggformula)
library(broom)
library(janitor)
```
:::


# Data

Dr. F is a full on wierdo and has been keeping a spreadsheet of every cup of coffee he's brewed at home since January 2026 with the hope of learning how to brew the perfect cup. He records the beans and roaster, the brewing method and setup, and his own ratings of how the cup turned out. Each row in the data set is a single cup of coffee.
The cell below contains code that reads the data into R from a Google Sheet and cleans up the column names. You will need to run this code to get started.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
coffee <- read_csv("https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/e/2PACX-1vTI_1K-jHHdbpi11aB5tX3Avr2eRXULfLg9BoveWhId4soH1BAnIJWuu0CiqO7YiP1TVoqsGi6kLbli/pub?gid=0&single=true&output=csv") |>
  clean_names()
```
:::


The full spreadsheet has many columns recording things like grind size, water temperature, and bean-to-water ratio. This homework will focus on just a handful of them:

- `vibes_rating`: Dr. F's overall rating of the cup, on a scale from 0 to 10.
- `roast`: the roast level of the beans (`Light`, `Light-Medium`, `Medium`, or `Dark`).
- `origin`: the country (or countries) the beans came from.
- `brewer`: the device used to brew the cup (e.g. Aeropress, V60).
- `aroma`, `sweetness`, `acidity`, `body`, `finish`: sub-scores, also on a 0 to 10 scale, that Dr. F assigns to specific aspects of the cup's flavor.

::: callout-note
You'll also see a column called `average`. It's calculated from the five flavor sub-scores (`aroma`, `sweetness`, `acidity`, `body`, `finish`) and, unsurprisingly, is strongly related to `vibes_rating`. Because it's essentially a summary of variables we're already using, **don't use `average` anywhere in this analysis**: it won't tell you anything the other variables don't already show.
:::

## Analysis goal

What makes a good cup of coffee, at least by Dr. F's own standards? The goal of this analysis is to explore how the beans, brewing choices, and flavor characteristics of a cup relate to how highly Dr. F rated it.

# Exercises

Write all code and narrative in your Quarto file. Write all narrative in complete sentences.
Remember to write all of your responses in an answer box below, there should be a one created for each exercise already.
Throughout the assignment, you should periodically **render** your Quarto document to produce the updated HTML file.

::: {.exercise}
Run the code below to get a first look at the `coffee` data set.
Create a code chunk in the answer block below and write a little bit of code to take quick look at your data so you can answer these questions.
How many observations (rows) and how many variables (columns) does `coffee` have? In your own words, what does a single row of this data set represent?
:::

::: {.answer}

:::

::: {.exercise}
Using the variable descriptions above, classify each of the following as **quantitative** or **categorical**: `vibes_rating`, `roast`, `origin`, `brewer`, `aroma`, `sweetness`, `acidity`, `body`, `finish`.
:::

::: {.answer}

:::

::: {.exercise}
In the console, look at the distinct roast levels with `unique(coffee$roast)`. By default, R will treat `roast` as a plain character variable, which means any plot involving `roast` will list the levels alphabetically: `Dark`, `Light`, `Light-Medium`, `Medium`.

Fill in the blanks below to turn `roast` into a **factor** with its levels ordered from lightest to darkest roast instead. Make sure you change `eval: FALSE` to `eval: TRUE`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
coffee <- coffee |>
  mutate(roast = factor(roast, levels = c("____", "____", "____", "____")))
```
:::


Why is R's default alphabetical ordering a problem for a variable like `roast`? What would change in a bar chart of `roast` once the levels are ordered from lightest to darkest?
:::

::: {.answer}

:::

::: {.exercise}
Let's look at the distribution of our response variable, `vibes_rating`. Fill in the blanks below to create a histogram with an informative title and axis labels, and to calculate summary statistics using the `favstats` function from the `mosaic` package. Remember to change `eval: FALSE` to `eval: TRUE`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gf_histogram(~____, data = ____) |>
  gf_labs(x = "_____",
       y = "_____",
       title = "_____")

favstats(~____, data = ____)
```
:::


Use the plot and summary statistics to describe the distribution of `vibes_rating`: comment on its shape, center, spread, and the presence of any apparent outliers.

::: callout-tip
This is a good place to render your document.
:::
:::

::: {.answer}

:::

::: {.exercise}
Now let's look at the categorical variables. The code below makes a bar chart of `roast`:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gf_bar(~roast, data = coffee) |>
  gf_labs(x = "Roast",
       y = "Count",
       title = "Distribution of Roast Levels")
```
:::


Using this as a template, create your own bar charts for `origin` and `brewer`, each with an informative title and axis labels.

Which roast, origin, and brewer show up most often in Dr. F's coffee log? You'll likely notice that `origin` has some messy categories (for example, blends of multiple countries) and that some categories have only a handful of cups. Why is that worth keeping in mind for the comparisons we'll make in the rest of this homework?
:::

::: {.answer}


::: {.cell}

:::


:::

::: {.exercise}
Now examine the distributions of the five flavor sub-scores: `aroma`, `sweetness`, `acidity`, `body`, and `finish`. For each one, create a histogram (with an informative title and axis labels) and calculate summary statistics with `favstats`.

Based on your plots and summary statistics: which sub-score does Dr. F tend to rate highest on average? Which does he rate lowest? Which sub-score varies the most from cup to cup? (_Hint: look at the standard deviation, `sd`, in the `favstats` output._)
:::

::: {.answer}


::: {.cell}

:::


:::

::: {.exercise}
Let's compare `vibes_rating` across categories. Create side-by-side boxplots of `vibes_rating` by `roast`, and another set by `origin`. For each, also calculate `favstats(vibes_rating ~ roast, data = coffee)` (and the equivalent for `origin`).

Based on your plots and summary statistics, what kinds of roasts does Dr. F seem to prefer? What about origins? Be sure to reference the group sizes (`n` in the `favstats` output): how much should we trust a comparison built on a group with only two or three cups?
:::

::: {.answer}


::: {.cell}

:::


:::

::: {.exercise}
Now do the same comparison for `brewer`: a side-by-side boxplot of `vibes_rating` by `brewer`, plus the corresponding `favstats`.

Even if one brewer has a noticeably higher mean rating, explain why we can't conclude that brewer _causes_ better-tasting coffee. (_Hint: over the months represented in this data set, did Dr. F change anything else besides the brewer, such as the beans, roaster, or grinder, at around the same time he changed brewers?_)

::: callout-tip
This is another good place to render your document.
:::
:::

::: {.answer}


::: {.cell}

:::


:::

::: {.exercise}
Finally, let's look at how `vibes_rating` relates to the flavor sub-scores. Choose at least three of `aroma`, `sweetness`, `acidity`, `body`, and `finish`. For each one, create a scatterplot of `vibes_rating` against that sub-score with a fitted line (e.g. `gf_point(vibes_rating ~ acidity, data = coffee) |> gf_lm()`), and calculate the correlation (e.g. `cor(vibes_rating ~ acidity, data = coffee, use = "complete.obs")`).

::: callout-note
A few cups are missing a rating, so `cor()` needs to be told to skip those rows: that's what the `use = "complete.obs"` argument does. Without it, `cor()` will return `NA`.
:::

For each relationship, describe its direction, form, and strength. Then answer the analysis question from the beginning of this homework: based on what you found, which flavor characteristic seems to matter most to Dr. F's overall rating? Which one seems to matter least?
:::

::: {.answer}


::: {.cell}

:::


:::

::: {.exercise}
So far we've looked at variables two at a time. Now create one plot that shows **three** variables at once, for example:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gf_point(vibes_rating ~ acidity, color = ~roast, data = coffee)
```
:::


Does the relationship between `vibes_rating` and the sub-score you chose look the same within each roast level as it did overall in the previous exercise, or does it change depending on the roast? (_This is the same idea behind Simpson's Paradox from lecture: a relationship between two variables can look different, or even reverse, once you account for a third._)

Finally, in a short paragraph, discuss the limitations of this data set. Dr. F chose which cups to brew, brewed them himself, and rated them himself, usually knowing which beans and method he was using at the time. Given that, what can this analysis reasonably tell us, and what can't it tell us?
:::

::: {.answer}

